 some that Reiki is an ancient  healing method it was actually found  in the early 20th century by the  Buddhist monk Macau Tsui in the 20s  Sooey fasted and meditated for 21 days  on Mount Kurama in Japan and when he  emerged from this seclusion he claimed  that he received a revelation of an  ancient Tibetan practice which we now  know as Reiki it's been claimed that  asou a was a Christian priest and had a  doctorate in theology none of that's  true and was actually made up by Reiki  practitioners during the 20th century in  order to ingratiate themselves with  American Christians and in reality who  suya was a missionary of a Shintoism  related religion  so again advocates claim that the  practice is ancient and that use of  Reiki energy has been around for at  least a thousand years and according to  Reiki org quote some feel it has been  around since the beginning of time there  is no proof that who Suey rediscovered  anything ancient he actually created  Reiki by borrowing bits and pieces from  Japanese and Buddhist traditions and  then rebranded them as something new  right now no special background or  credentials are needed to become a Reiki  practitioner you need only an atonement  from a master and training for the basic  levels of attunement can take as little  as one to two days very efficient but  years of training are needed to reach  these supposed highest levels no  licensing is required and interestingly  surprising to some people interest in  Reiki is quite large and it keeps  growing according to a CDC survey in  2006 1.2 million adults and a hundred  and sixty one thousand kids in the US  received one or more energy healing  sessions like Reiki and Reiki education  is offered across the country at roughly  eight hundred hospitals and more than 60  hospitals explicitly offer Reiki to the  patients themselves very often the  hospitals end up offering Reiki to the  patients not because the hospital  necessarily believes that Reiki works or  because there's definitive science  behind it but because it's the patient  that demand reiki there was a 2008 study  of hospitals that utilize touch healing  practices such as Reiki which found that  84% sighted patient demand as the  primary reason for offering these  services the cleveland clinic which is a  non-profit academic Medical Center in  Cleveland Ohio is one such hospital they  advertise Reiki for the treatment of  cancer chronic pain infertility  digestive problems parkinson's disease  at the University of Arizona Medical  Center they offer cancer patients all  sorts of quack Adamic treatments  including Reiki and they tell the  patients that those treatments can boost  your immune system and complement  conventional chemotherapy the benefits  are not well enumerated and it's unclear  how Reiki could actually help with any  of those conditions instead of offering  it as a replacement for actual treatment  practitioners of Reiki and the hospitals  and the medical facilities that offer it  will typically recommend integrating  Reiki into whatever treatment plan the  patient is already undergoing and to  provide a veneer of scientific  legitimacy Reiki defenders talk about  something called quantum entanglement  which describes a phenomenon in quantum  physics when pairs of group or groups of  particles act upon each other and become  an entangled system and defenders of  Reiki used this as a pretext to claim  that Reiki practitioners can affect  others on a quantum scale it's tough to  really pin them down but ultimately the  scientific justification boils down to  Reiki works because quantum physics and  that's more or less the beginning and  end of the argument there is a 2011  paper which investigated the difference  between treatment by a certified Reiki  practitioner no intervention and a  placebo practitioner right someone who  was not trained in Reiki both the quote  real Reiki and the fake Reiki performed  better than the no intervention group  but the real and fake Reiki performed  exactly the same so to put it simply in  that study Rey keys effects were the  placebo effect not uncommon in  pseudoscience in 2008 the Journal of  alternative and complementary medicine  published a randomized controlled trial  of Rey keys effects on fibromyalgia pain  and it stated plainly that quote neither  reiki nor touch improved the symptoms of  fibromyalgia in 2008 in the  international journal of clinical  practices published a review entitled  effects of reiki in clinical practice a  systematic review of randomized clinical  trials and came to the following  conclusion quote the evidence is  insufficient to suggest that reiki is an  effective treatment for any condition  therefore the value of reiki remains  unproven the comprehensive literature  reviews are unable to say with precision  whether or not reiki is able to work  hypothetically largely because the  studies available for reviews are almost  entirely bad science the few good  experiments that exist like the ones i  cited are vastly outweighed by the  number of studies that are set up  specifically to prove that reiki is real  although the science behind reiki is  thin interest in reiki as I mentioned  keeps growing Ricky's influence has  actually grown so large that in 2009 the  Catholic Church felt the need to release  official doctrine regarding the  acceptability of Reiki for Catholics and  Catholic hospitals and the reason for  doing this as far as they said was  because of a strong demand from clergy  and Catholics more generally for Reiki  and the report was unequivocal saying  quote Reiki therapy is not compatible  with either Christian teaching or  scientific evidence it would be  inappropriate for Catholic institutions  to promote or to provide support for  Reiki therapy a Catholic who puts his or  her trust in Reiki would be operating in  the realm of superstition this is from  an institution the Catholic Church it  still offers classes at the Vatican on